#
	# Introduction to `Tindalwic`
	
	In fourteen examples...

{comment block}
	#
		This example has only a prolog comment block.
		Comments are [Github Flavored Markdown](https://github.github.com/gfm/)

{comment shortcut}
	# you can skip the block if there's just one line

{comment flow}
	# if you really want to you can flow from the first line into a block...
		but that can obscure the GFM: this whole block looks like it is a code block
		
		    this is actually a code block because it has four spaces more than the text
		
		tindalwic consumes the margin before sending the content on to GFM

{warning about shortcut comments}
	# they can't follow values, the # must always come right after the margin
		{writing something like this} # is tempting but not supported (yet?)

{curly brackets surround keys and open a nested dictionary}
	# you've seen several of these already - all the examples are dictionaries
	{dictionaries can nest}
		{as deep as you need them to go}
			{}
				# the empty string is a valid key
		{{{no quoting/escaping, only the 1st and last chars are brackets}}}

{square brackets surround keys and open a nested list}
	[lists entries in dictionaries have keys]
		# but keys aren't a thing for list items so the brackets surround nothing
		{}
			[]
			[previous entry's key was empty]
				[]
		[]

{angle brackets surround keys and open a text block}
	<key for the text>
		these are very similar to comments, but GFM is not assumed, and can't flow
		text values are, of course, leafs so nothing can nest inside it:
		{this is just text}
		the content is verbatim UTF-8, quoting/escaping isn't needed.

{the first char of a text block can be a TAB}
	<key>
			<- like this.
		indentation is always TABs, never spaces, and increases strictly monotonically.
		other syntaxes are more flexible, but they all need quoting/escaping mechanisms.

{epilog comments for text}
	<key>
		# this is text, not a comment (verbatim, no quoting/escaping).
		there's no room in the block for anything but text.
	# epilog comments can follow the block so you can describe a text values
		they dedent back to the same level as the brackets

{shortcut for single-line text dictionary entries}
	{dict}
		# two ways to write the same entry:
		key=value
		<key>
			value
		# spaces are not (yet?) stripped, so this is different:
		key = value
		# that key is 4 chars long

{shortcut for single-line text list items}
	[list]
		# two ways to write the same item:
		value
		<>
			value

{epilog comments are not just for text}
	# dedent-and-follow works for dictionaries and lists as well
	{dict}
		# prolog for dict
		key=value
		# epilog for value
	# epilog for dict
	[list]
		# prolog for list
		item
		# epilog for item
	# epilog for list

{keys aren't limited to single-line text}
	@
		use an "at" sign to contain a block of text
		the LF chars between the lines is part of the key
		but, of course, not the margin
	<>
		and the value follows the key block, looking like a list item
		it would be invalid syntax for the brackets to surround any chars

{keys can also have comments}
	# they can't use # or they would be ambiguous with prolog comments like this, so

	// key comments precede the entry and use the double-slash syntax
		they flow, and they can follow a blank line. WARNING: completely blank lines in
		other places confuse the parser. the 3rd line of this file, for example, looks blank,
		but it needs to have the indentation or it confuses the parsers. I'll fix that ASAP.
	key1=value1

	key2=value2
	# as you may have noticed already, a blank line is allowed even without a comment

	// they work the same way for all the key flavors
	<key3>
		value3
